Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard, and with me today, RevenueCat CEO, Jacob Iting.

6
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:51,200
Our guest today is Jesse Venticinque, co-founder and Chief Product Officer at Fitbot.

7
00:00:51,840 --> 00:01:04,400
On the podcast, we talk with Jesse about the trap of building for existing subscribers, incentivizing word of mouth, and why paid marketing should be an accelerator, not the foundation of your growth strategy.

8
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:07,600
Hey Jesse, thank you so much for joining us on the podcast today.

9
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:08,800
David, thanks for having me.

10
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:10,880
And Jacob, always nice to chat with you.

11
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:13,440
As always, here excited apps.

12
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:14,160
Let's go.

13
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:17,760
So Jesse, I am stoked to have you on the podcast.

14
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:27,840
I often gush about our guests because I try to get people on whose apps I use or, you know, businesses I admire and things like that, and I'm a huge Fitbot fanboy.

15
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:35,440
So you'll hear me gushing throughout the whole podcast because I was looking for a weightlifting app earlier this year and just fell in love and have been using it weekly since.

16
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:37,440
So it's gonna be fun chatting today.

17
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:38,960
I did want to kick things off.

18
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:42,560
So as I was looking at Fitbot as a.

19
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:52,720
As a subscription nerd, I saw that you all have passed twenty million dollars in ARR and are profitable and are venture backed.

20
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:59,120
So three interesting, not necessarily expected things to combine into one.

21
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:01,600
So it must be quite a journey so far.

22
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:07,120
So tell us a little bit about founding Fitpod and raising capital and kind of getting to this point.

23
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:08,000
Sure thing.

24
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,720
And yeah, David, thanks for the support and the praise.

25
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:16,000
Yeah, I think there are several factors that have allowed us to grow the business largely on our revenues alone.

26
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:18,000
I think two of the most important ones.

27
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:23,680
The first is a maniacal focus on product retention on building something that people want.

28
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:38,080
So Alan, my cofounder and I, we spent the first several years just the two of us building product, listening to customers before hiring, before fundraising, before marketing and really trying to leverage an engaged user base to spread the word.

29
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:44,480
And once we did turn on paid advertising, the unit economics were pretty immediately scalable.

30
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:50,640
I think particularly because of our strong conversion and retention or LTV, which kind of enabled fundraising enabled.

31
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:58,320
hiring and the paid marketing really acts as an accelerator to our word of mouth viral loop.

32
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:03,200
And so like as we ramped our paid subscribers, our organic subs kind of grew in line.

33
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:06,960
And maybe lastly about product is around recurring revenue.

34
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:15,440
So our subscription like retention curve flattens out and we have users at 36 months, 48 months, even like 60 months that are still paying us.

35
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:21,760
So each new cohort of users we acquire adds to this stack of recurring revenue, which is fundamentally about product.

36
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:24,640
I think the second reason our goal with.

37
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:30,320
FitBod to challenge the fitness status quo kind of mirrors our approach to company building.

38
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:40,640
So the fitness industry and brands, there's a lot of snake oil, a lot of shortcuts to success, like the six week plan or this new gadget to promise you the best shape of your life, look perfect.

39
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:44,560
The truth is that physical fitness should be a lifelong practice.

40
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:47,600
And, you know, especially after we're in the best shape of our lives, right?

41
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,840
Like as we age, it's even more critical to do this.

42
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,080
And this is kind of a quip, but my fitness goal is to be like a kick ass hundred year old.

43
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:57,040
Like I want to kick a soccer ball to like my great-nep, right?

44
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,400
And so we built a worko a workout app with only one workout, it's your next one.

45
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:02,240
In a sense, it's kind of your lifelong workout.

46
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:03,280
It kind of meets you where you are.

47
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:13,120
So similar to startups, especially in the past several years, the past ten years, there's been this culture of using shortcuts to hit some idealized milestone, like raising and spending money prior to product market fit.

48
00:04:13,360 --> 00:04:15,520
So I think it was kind of less sexy at the time.

49
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:20,640
Now it's a little more popular the path we took, but it's almost like this kind of fitness ideology.

50
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:24,000
Results take time and work, and we got a composite success.

51
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:28,400
Yeah, that's such an incredible start to an app like this.

52
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:34,720
I didn't realize that you all had spent so much time dialing in some level of product market fit before you raised.

53
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:38,000
What did that very early journey look like?

54
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:41,840
Did you launch the app and actually start getting users?

55
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:48,480
And did you have a little angel funding or was this like a side project before you found the product market fit and then eventually raised some funding?

56
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:51,520
My co-founder Alan and I, my background is in product design.

57
00:04:51,760 --> 00:05:01,200
I had worked as a freelancer for his previous startup and Fitbot did start out, after that one wound down, we did start it as a side project and really solving like a personal problem.

58
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:06,480
And I think we had a very unique approach to solving the problem, which we can get into a little bit later.

59
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:12,640
But yeah, we put it into the App Store and immediately saw a little bit of traction that I think kind of just built on itself.

60
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:19,840
We did have like an early beta testing phase where we did make an important pivot from more of an advanced tracker to a recommended workout plan.

61
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:28,400
And you know, I think ASO App Store optimization actually was pretty important in the beginning, being able to kind of rank in Apple search results.

62
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:35,840
Alan Morso is the engineer of the analytical approach and he really spent time trying to like test keywords and ranking in those early days.

63
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:38,240
So I think a lot of early users found us that way.

64
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:39,040
It was really interesting.

65
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:45,920
It was kind of when users were like us unilaterally emailing us and that email was growing over time of feedback and requests and praise.

66
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:48,160
That was kind of the first inkling of product market fit.

67
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:51,920
What was the actual tipping point then of like, now we're really on something.

68
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:55,600
We're going to raise some funding, start hiring and like, this is a real business.

69
00:05:56,480 --> 00:06:04,400
It was probably when we first monetized, which was still maybe a year and a half before we closed our first seed round, started monetizing.

70
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:10,640
And, you know, this was 2016 as consumer SaaS or subscription was kind of early, right?

71
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:19,520
And it was surprising to us, at least, that someone would pay a subscription for kind of a utilitarian product that wasn't necessarily like a content based.

72
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:21,840
So it's like, okay, people are paying us for this thing.

73
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:30,640
And then looking up and realizing like, hey, our unique approach leveraging AI and ML to produce like these customized strength training prescriptions, it seems very scalable.

74
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:34,240
It's a big problem out there, and we have this unique tech.

75
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:36,480
So that was kind of how we gained confidence.

76
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:44,560
Was there, and this is like, I guess I'm asking for the founders like in this situation, and it's a common dilemma is like, well, can I get this thing off the ground?

77
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:46,160
Do I need to bring on outside capital?

78
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:48,400
Because, you know, the truth is you bring on outside capital.

79
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:50,240
When you're trying to get on outside capital, you have to unload outside capital, right?

80
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:56,400
It puts you on this trajectory, which there are variations to that path, but it starts to narrow your potential future options.

81
00:06:57,040 --> 00:06:58,560
What was the big impetus for you guys?

82
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:07,280
Like, it was like, hey, like, we want to be able to go faster or was it, hey, we want this to be venture scale or what was the decision making and was it even a decision?

83
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:11,520
Was this like, we're going to do this and now we're able to or what was your thought process?

84
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:17,600
There was a question of whether to run this as kind of a small business or raise funding, right?

85
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:21,680
And as you mentioned, like when you raise from investors, the game changes quite a bit.

86
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:28,800
The early product market fit and just the prospect of being able to scale like a very underrated form of exercise.

87
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:34,000
Like I think strength training is the secret hiding in plain sight and it really could make a really dramatic impact.

88
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:36,560
I think it's kind of what the world needs in a sense.

89
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:42,640
If you think about it more broadly, maybe with health care, right, and freedom from pain, being able to move about the world, right.

90
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:45,600
I think there's some fundamental ideas there that we got really excited about.

91
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:50,960
And I think we found a great initial partner in Jason Kalicanis to lead that seed round and get it going.

92
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:52,480
That's the guy with the podcast.

93
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:53,680
That's right.

94
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:53,920
Yeah.

95
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:54,560
The podcast.

96
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:55,200
Uh huh.

97
00:07:56,480 --> 00:08:13,920
With money in the bank and a profitable company, you mentioned that paid marketing has been a factor in growth and that because you have really good fundamental numbers that was an accelerator, was there pressure to just go raise fifty million dollars and blow it up with as much paid marketing as possible?

98
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:16,080
And how did you navigate that landscape?

99
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:21,280
I imagine venture capitalists were just wanting to throw money at you in that phase of funding.

100
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:24,400
All founders are required to say yes, whether or not it's true or not.

101
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:35,040
You know, when we launched in 2015, fitness tech wasn't as popular as it was today, I think My Fitness PAL was probably the most successful outcome at like 500 million dollars.

102
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:39,120
But, you know, and now look at the landscape in the last couple of years, it's become very popular.

103
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:51,600
So we were kind of in an underdog industry at the beginning and Alan and I internally, we recognize the downside potentially and we basically found investors who aligned with that.

104
00:08:51,840 --> 00:09:01,760
So Jason has always kind of run against the grain of the grow at all costs and our subsequent rounds, they've largely been made up of startup operators who have aligned with our model.

105
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:14,400
So, for example, like the co-founders of Calm, the meditation app App, who famously beat Headspace by staying lean, and another investor is an operator at Notion, who I think once described revenue per headcount as a great metric.

106
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:29,040
I mean, that's I think often overlooked, especially in the frenzy of fundraising, is that like alignment between your investors and it's sometimes personality and background when you're talking about seed and angel stage, and also just like structure of what their funds are designed to do.

107
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:31,600
Like who did they raise money from and the expectations they set?

108
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:32,720
I mean, do they have a thesis?

109
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:37,680
And is there something about what you're doing that goes beyond just like short term returns, right?

110
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:49,520
And I think this is where folks not to dwell too much on the venture capital side of things, but I think this is where some folks can end up in the horror stories you hear is when you take money and there's misalignment in how that money is deployed.

111
00:09:49,760 --> 00:10:00,720
When there's a lack of trust and a lack of shared vision, it's worse than a bad marriage because like it's really hard to unwind a venture capital deal, like it takes a little bit of luck, but also just picking wisely, right?

112
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:03,920
Think about who you raise money from more even than who you hire.

113
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:08,560
There's some advice out there that's like money is money and there's some truth in that.

114
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:12,720
But like I do think you can go a lot further and your life will be a lot better if you choose.

115
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:20,160
wisely and if you're blessed with a good product and like leverage right in the process, which I mean, the way you guys did it was right.

116
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:24,000
Don't show up to your first pitch with a slide deck of what an app could look like.

117
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:24,320
Right.

118
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:25,840
And three test flight users, right?

119
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:28,960
Like, show up with like a basics of a flywheel.

120
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:36,000
I feel like with consumer and consumer subscription specifically, you almost need to be a stage ahead of where like a B to B company would have to be.

121
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:36,240
Right.

122
00:10:36,400 --> 00:10:38,320
Like, you guys have to be proving scalability.

123
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:41,520
You need to have product market fit at the seed versus like a B to B.

124
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:42,640
It's at the A usually.

125
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:48,720
And you need to have scalability at the A, which a B to B company typically wouldically won't have until like the B or C.

126
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:50,000
It's definitely harder.

127
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:53,280
I'm sure we'll come back to it, but I think the concepts of community we talk about this.

128
00:10:53,440 --> 00:11:03,680
Every time we get someone on here with a successful app, there's some aspect of we had some magic substrate, some ether that allowed us to amplify our spend.

129
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:08,960
And as a venture capitalist, when you hear that, you're like, you start to hear like, oh, I hear alpha, right?

130
00:11:09,120 --> 00:11:10,720
Like, I hear return, I hear margin.

131
00:11:11,280 --> 00:11:13,680
And so I think that makes the decision a lot easier.

132
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:23,120
Like, I think in consumer, especially, and just to continue to preach, but like, I definitely caution founders don't fundraising until like you're, I think, kind of at that stage.

133
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:24,800
Like you're at that postpartum market fit.

134
00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:27,040
You got some scale and like you know you really want to go for it.

135
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:27,920
That's really cool.

136
00:11:28,320 --> 00:11:28,400
Yeah.

137
00:11:28,640 --> 00:11:42,080
And so that's where I actually wanted to go next is that because you chose and of course in hindsight in 2023, you look like, you know, rock stars with a $20 million ARR profitable, you know, couldn't be better positioned.

138
00:11:42,400 --> 00:11:47,680
And as a customer, I'm thrilled to have heard that, you know, I know this is going to be a product I can rely on for years to come.

139
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:51,760
And you're going to build towards not just an exit, but towards a great customer experience.

140
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:54,560
So all those things I'm excited about, but choosing.

141
00:11:55,120 --> 00:11:59,520
not just dumping a pile of cash on the app meant you had to go in other directions.

142
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:05,600
So, apart from the paid marketing you have done, what have those sources of traffic been for growth?

143
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:09,440
So I mentioned the word of mouth and kind of the viral growth loop.

144
00:12:09,920 --> 00:12:13,680
So that's kind of critical and still actually, I think, a major growth driver today.

145
00:12:14,320 --> 00:12:25,840
And so we have a whole strategy set out to try to identify and focus on which step of that viral growth loop is the highest leverage to move a top level metric.

146
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,800
And if we do take word of mouth virality, that top level metric.

147
00:12:29,440 --> 00:12:34,080
is like the number of new subscribers that an existing subscriber will generate.

148
00:12:34,240 --> 00:12:45,760
Our loop kind of looks like a new user comes in, they become engaged with the product, they tell another user via word of mouth or say they can share their fitness progress or results, and that will invite another user who becomes engaged.

149
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:53,280
And product being kind of foundational also comes into play here where word of mouth is enabled when a product beats the expectations for a new user, right?

150
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:55,840
They come up with some of the expectations and it totally blows them out of the water.

151
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,840
And that's what enables word of mouth.

152
00:12:58,160 --> 00:13:06,320
And were there specific ways that you worked as a product leader to kind of identify those loops.

153
00:13:06,560 --> 00:13:08,800
I'm a little burned on the growth loop term.

154
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:15,040
I mean, sometimes for some apps, it really is kind of magical thinking because it's just not the kind of app that is going to get virality.

155
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:22,000
But I think a lot of founders do themselves a service by not at least exploring ways that that could happen.

156
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:28,080
So I'd love to hear how FitBod identified and nurtured those growth loops.

157
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:31,360
To your point, we're not a naturally social product.

158
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,720
So I think the viral growth loop is important.

159
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,480
In other words, like, one just can't use paid UA, right?

160
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:42,640
Paid UA is kind of a law of the universe that the more you spend, the less efficient.

161
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:44,960
And, you know, paid UA is kind of like a funnel, right?

162
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:47,600
Where the more you put in the top, the more you might get out the bottom.

163
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:49,840
But it's not necessarily like a compounding loop.

164
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:56,320
So I think for us, virality is an important part of the growth story, but we probably need to add other growth loops as well.

165
00:13:56,480 --> 00:14:00,960
And content being another one, for example, could be user generated or, for example, our blog.

166
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:03,200
Our blog is something we've been investing in quite a bit.

167
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:07,120
And there's kind of a obviously scalable idea there.

168
00:14:07,600 --> 00:14:12,000
We produce content that will drive new users who will produce more revenue to invest in more content.

169
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:15,120
So I see it as like several growth groups playing together.

170
00:14:15,360 --> 00:14:16,880
We're not a naturally viral product, right?

171
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:24,320
So it's maybe unlikely for us to hit a viral coefficient where a new subscriber will invite more than one new subscriber, unless, you know, but you don't need to, right?

172
00:14:24,560 --> 00:14:31,120
You're not NGL or one of these, you know, social apps that lives or dies by social virality or gas or one of these others.

173
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:36,160
But the way that you were saying is like that long term retention, especially with your long view on it, right?

174
00:14:36,320 --> 00:14:42,480
Which I think the retention curve of your subscribers will map the value curve derived, right?

175
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:47,200
So like as long as users are continuously proceediving value that that cohort will not go to zero.

176
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:49,360
And that means usage and engagement.

177
00:14:49,600 --> 00:14:55,360
The thing you said was really profound was about incentivizing virality or word of mouth through beating expectations.

178
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:56,800
That's really fascinating.

179
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:58,800
I haven't thought about it that way, but you're right.

180
00:14:59,120 --> 00:15:02,640
You come in and it ties in to the investor argument about seeking alpha, right?

181
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:10,160
Like when a user comes in and gets more than they expect, that's information that's inherently valuable to other humans looking to exploit value, right?

182
00:15:10,320 --> 00:15:13,760
It's like find where can I find something where the perceived value is higher than the cost?

183
00:15:14,640 --> 00:15:30,800
I imagine you put that into the design of the app and like the user experience., like from a signup and macro level, like, were there specific things where you're like trying to, like, how do I drive this user to a viral moment within like their first month or like, how do you balance, I guess, maybe a different way of phrasing is like, how do you balance that?

184
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:40,480
And I guess maybe they're not in conflict, but those features or those things that are like, purely focused on this, like, long term fitness journey versus, like, well, also like, I want it to be so good you tell your friends, right?

185
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:45,360
This kind of like, segue into the idea of activation and the product hook.

186
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:51,040
So like a product hook is this kind of discrete, repeatable action that a user will take on a product.

187
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:52,960
And I think a lot of great consumer companies have have this.

188
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:56,160
They know there's like Google Search and I don't know, like Hanley and Uber.

189
00:15:56,800 --> 00:16:04,720
And we like, purposely designed our product hook or at least we took inspiration from Near Iall habit creation model in this.

190
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:06,880
And he had a 2012 book called Hooked.

191
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:12,960
And basically there's a trigger, an action, a reward, and an investment part of this habit creation loop.

192
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:20,640
And let's say for Fitbot, there's the trigger is some type of, you know, psychological uncertainty about what to do in the gym or how to strength train.

193
00:16:20,880 --> 00:16:24,240
And the action is launching the app and we solve that problem with your next workout.

194
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:31,440
Weut, we kind of celebrate it with various achievements and signals of progress.

195
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:37,360
And then the investment phase, you know, the user will invest their time or money or data into the product.

196
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:50,640
And so making that reward visible and shareable, which we've invested in quite a bit, I think has been important for a virality and allowing our customers to like show the value they're getting from the product.

197
00:16:51,280 --> 00:16:59,200
And so our customers will share their fitness progress out and a potential user will view that and understand the value or might think, hey, I could drive that value too.

198
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:03,120
There are some really fun things in the app that I've been really impressed with.

199
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:13,280
It's fun because when I use subscription apps now, I'm using it both as a user and delighted as a user with the user experience, but then also thinking about the subscription strategy.

200
00:17:14,080 --> 00:17:16,960
And I love the way the sharing screens are designed.

201
00:17:17,200 --> 00:17:22,720
And like one of the cool things you'll do in the sharing screen is show the total pounds lifted in a workout.

202
00:17:22,800 --> 00:17:24,160
And it's so freaking impressive.

203
00:17:24,320 --> 00:17:25,280
I've never seen that before.

204
00:17:25,680 --> 00:17:31,040
But like my last workout Monday with Fitbot, I lifted twenty thousand pounds or something.

205
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:33,200
And I mean, I'm not especially strong.

206
00:17:33,360 --> 00:17:36,160
I'm not like benching 300 or anything like that.

207
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:40,640
But it was a really impressive number and it's like I kind of wanted to share that.

208
00:17:40,880 --> 00:17:41,680
That's pretty cool.

209
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:47,920
But then something else you'll do too is that there's this almost constant banner of share six free workouts.

210
00:17:48,160 --> 00:17:56,320
Has that been a big driver of that referral thing where you normally get three free workouts, but if you share the link, your friend gets six free workouts.

211
00:17:56,560 --> 00:17:57,840
Has that driven a lot of users?

212
00:17:58,480 --> 00:17:58,720
It has.

213
00:17:58,960 --> 00:18:00,720
However, we haven't invested a ton into it.

214
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:05,760
And that's kind of an immediate strategy to put more in there referral six free workouts link there.

215
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:08,560
We actually added it to be able to under.

216
00:18:08,560 --> 00:18:11,200
to be able to understand and measure this growth loop.

217
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:15,040
So like the first step is to measure, right, and identify which part of that to focus on.

218
00:18:15,360 --> 00:18:28,480
And so we built that feature and with all the steps instrumentated, and we can know like what percentage of subscribers view the referral feature and then what percentage will send a referral, what percentage of recipients will tap on it and redeem and convert.

219
00:18:28,880 --> 00:18:33,280
And so the next step here is to identify which part of the conversion funnel would produce the highest outcome.

220
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:38,080
So it may be the case that we just need to increase the number of times a subscriber sees the referral page.

221
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:42,240
Part of the best, highest leverage part of the funnel is having a referred user convert, right?

222
00:18:42,800 --> 00:18:44,560
And we some type of incentive there.

223
00:18:44,800 --> 00:18:54,880
So, you know, now a lot of word of mouth happens outside of the product and we can work on trying to incentivize more of it happening in the product, but just, you know, knowing where to invest our limited time and resources on which part of the funnel.

224
00:18:55,120 --> 00:19:00,080
And this is true for the entire acquisition, activation, retention user life cycle.

225
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:08,400
I mean, it's interesting to know how many people do that referral motion, but, yeah, more interestingly, it's probably linearly related to the amount of word of mouth period.

226
00:19:08,720 --> 00:19:13,280
It's a very easy and obvious way to do a referral because word of mouth is extremely hard to track.

227
00:19:13,440 --> 00:19:16,640
You don't know, like you can ask people, it's imperfect, you can whatever.

228
00:19:17,200 --> 00:19:19,760
But let's say you're trying to like increase word of mouth.

229
00:19:19,920 --> 00:19:26,480
I would say, like, you could look at the result, like, how many people are referring as a good proxy to your other K factor, like inputs.

230
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:34,400
And I think that's something that I think I suffered from this in the past when I was working on consumer apps was thinking like, I have to measure everything properly.

231
00:19:34,480 --> 00:19:36,880
Like, if it's not instrumentated, I can't trust anything.

232
00:19:37,520 --> 00:19:41,040
The truth is, it's like getting perfect instrumentation is almost impossible.

233
00:19:41,520 --> 00:19:47,280
And you should be good enough with some things that you can plausibly say are going to be related.

234
00:19:47,600 --> 00:19:50,480
Then work on obvious, not necessarily obvious stuff, but like you can.

235
00:19:51,040 --> 00:19:55,120
work on things and look at the results of that proxy number and that can be good enough.

236
00:19:55,520 --> 00:19:58,640
So I understand the struggle on that referral programs.

237
00:19:58,720 --> 00:19:59,280
They're tricky, right?

238
00:19:59,600 --> 00:20:02,560
They're like hard to balance and hard to track and they're tricky to set up.

239
00:20:02,720 --> 00:20:14,320
But even if that isn't a silver bullet, like in terms of that giving you a two X K factor, it's like if nothing else, it gives you data on what, like, is that what people call it the K factor, like the one point one users per user or whatever.

240
00:20:14,960 --> 00:20:15,520
It's interesting.

241
00:20:16,080 --> 00:20:20,480
One of the things you've mentioned several times now is how important retention is to fit by.

242
00:20:20,880 --> 00:20:28,560
So I'd love to explore how you think about retention and how you measure it retention, then we can talk more about how you drive that retention.

243
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:35,040
Going back to Jacob, your comment, instrumenting is hard and retention is one of the most challenging things for a few reasons.

244
00:20:35,760 --> 00:20:38,160
And so we break it down into different levels of fidelity.

245
00:20:38,320 --> 00:20:45,280
So like at the highest level, we have subscription retention, did a subscriber auto renew into the next period or not?

246
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:48,320
And with an annual plan, it takes a year to know that.

247
00:20:48,560 --> 00:20:50,480
I think with Consumer SaaS, we see this.

248
00:20:50,960 --> 00:20:57,920
There's likely a cohort of inactive renewers, subscribers who are disengaged with the product but will renew anyway.

249
00:20:58,560 --> 00:21:05,680
So say we want to increase subscription retention, if we reactivate those inactive renewers, it won't move the metric at all, right?

250
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:07,200
Because they're already paying for it.

251
00:21:07,360 --> 00:21:11,920
So under subscription retention, we look at cancellation retention.

252
00:21:12,240 --> 00:21:15,680
Did the subscriber hit the cancel button in the period or not?

253
00:21:15,920 --> 00:21:21,840
And that can be measured on a monthly basis because a subscriber cancel before their subscription period expires.

254
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:23,440
So when do they hit the cancel button?

255
00:21:23,760 --> 00:21:30,720
And the last level before that, kind of the most important, we have a workout retention metric, which is synonymous with engagement.

256
00:21:31,040 --> 00:21:32,720
But like a subscriber must log in.

257
00:21:34,080 --> 00:21:37,440
three plus workouts per month to survive to the following month.

258
00:21:37,760 --> 00:21:41,200
That's like the true retention we try to move and operate on to move the top level.

259
00:21:41,920 --> 00:21:44,160
You just said like most people do this at some level.

260
00:21:44,480 --> 00:21:52,800
So you just say like you choose a number, say like, hey, folks doing three or more, we think that's like a good, healthy, minimum level of engagement and you focus on that.

261
00:21:53,120 --> 00:21:58,080
You just chose that using your gut and informed decision or what, what's the reason for that cut off?

262
00:21:58,560 --> 00:22:00,400
Practically in the dashboard, you can change that number.

263
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:03,200
I can look at one plus, two plus, three plus, four plus.

264
00:22:03,760 --> 00:22:09,520
But, you know, if we look at like our ideal customer profile, the people who do show the best conversion and retention in the app.

265
00:22:09,840 --> 00:22:12,640
It's something like two point something workouts a week actually.

266
00:22:13,600 --> 00:22:13,680
Yeah.

267
00:22:14,080 --> 00:22:17,920
But again, it goes back to this concept of low resolution metrics.

268
00:22:18,640 --> 00:22:31,040
Like you just pick some things that you think are somewhat sensitive to what matters and then building a model of understanding exactly how those three workouts translate into paid subscription and then paid subscription retention is incredibly complicated.

269
00:22:31,600 --> 00:22:33,920
But you can imagine it probably helps, right?

270
00:22:34,320 --> 00:22:42,800
So it's like drive that and you can plausibly drive the next metric or drive down the cancellation metric or drive up the retention metric, which is interesting.

271
00:22:43,040 --> 00:22:47,760
I mean, the discussion around inactive subscribers is very interesting.

272
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:54,560
I think as an industry for us, you know, I think we've, I mean, it's sort of not our problem, but it's like something we benefit from.

273
00:22:54,720 --> 00:22:55,680
And it's an interesting thing.

274
00:22:55,840 --> 00:23:01,280
I think Apple's gotten more and they've lifted a lot of this for us.

275
00:23:01,520 --> 00:23:13,360
But like, you know, it would be interesting for Apple to create even more incentive for us to like notify users, like create a level, like if they went and started tracking activity in the apps and recommended people to unsubscribe, that might be really interesting.

276
00:23:13,600 --> 00:23:15,840
I think the emails, the monthly emails are pretty good.

277
00:23:15,920 --> 00:23:17,520
Like say, hey, you're subscribed to this.

278
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:34,400
It's like a good start, but it does create, I think, sometimes interesting incentive structures for us or people running apps in terms of like, if I send an email to this whole list, I might actually damage my subscription engine, which is, I think, probably morally what you want to do, but also like we're trying to run a business here, right?

279
00:23:34,640 --> 00:23:36,720
And like, I don't know, it's a tricky thing.

280
00:23:37,360 --> 00:23:40,560
But again, I think it's like focus on those usage, right?

281
00:23:40,720 --> 00:23:41,680
Like drive up usage.

282
00:23:41,760 --> 00:23:46,320
Like if you can drive up usage and retention, the score will take care of itself and a lot of this stuff.

283
00:23:46,880 --> 00:23:49,040
But optionality is a job to be done..

284
00:23:49,360 --> 00:23:50,720
I have the option to work out.

285
00:23:50,960 --> 00:23:53,840
I have a great workout app for that next workout I'm going to do.

286
00:23:54,400 --> 00:24:00,080
It's tough because you're not directly providing value in those situations, but in some ways you are.

287
00:24:00,960 --> 00:24:05,600
But what are some of the ways that Fitbot works to increase those numbers?

288
00:24:05,920 --> 00:24:13,200
Do you have specific in app or push or email campaigns around driving those workouts?

289
00:24:13,840 --> 00:24:20,720
The retention strategy is to focus on users who are becoming at risk or becoming dormant before they cancel.

290
00:24:21,040 --> 00:24:25,440
Typically, I think there's like lower leverageage and trying to revive churned users.

291
00:24:25,760 --> 00:24:29,840
The longer a user goes from using the product, it's probably harder to get them back.

292
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:38,000
So we're trying to pinpoint higher up in that funnel when a user is at risk of going dormant and try to use engagement tactics and interventions there.

293
00:24:38,480 --> 00:24:47,520
We have a model that predicts user engagement and going back to the other, Jacob, to your comment, I think logging workouts, we discovered is the best predictor of engagement.

294
00:24:47,840 --> 00:24:49,760
So yeah, we want to focus there on that area.

295
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:51,680
And what else could we potentially focus on?

296
00:24:51,840 --> 00:24:55,520
Well, we could look at our super active engaged users and build the features they're looking for.

297
00:24:56,080 --> 00:25:04,320
However, I think the bigger point of leverage that moved that metric is to try to understand why people are going dormant and try to solve that problem.

298
00:25:04,880 --> 00:25:06,240
And it goes back to the areas of focus.

299
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:08,800
It's probably a wider cohort too, right?

300
00:25:09,040 --> 00:25:14,000
Like, because you probably have a lot of folks that don't, maybe don't attach or like maybe only retain for a little while, right?

301
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:20,640
There's just probably more surface area in terms of like users in that cohort to attack than like your super hyper engaged users.

302
00:25:21,120 --> 00:25:27,280
You keep using the word leverage, which I think sometimes gets overused, but I think actually in this case, alpha is also the word, right?

303
00:25:27,520 --> 00:25:41,280
It's like where in your apps individual model is like going to be the most ROI and like talking about those different user bases is really fascinating because like sometimes I think the advice on product can be talk to your most like deeply engaged users, right?

304
00:25:41,440 --> 00:25:42,400
And like build what they want.

305
00:25:42,640 --> 00:25:50,400
And that's true to some extent, but maybe in consumer land where everyone's revenue is like capped, right?

306
00:25:50,640 --> 00:26:05,360
Like you're going to retain it for a long time in B to B, you know, if I can take a 10K account, make it a 100K account, make it a 500K account, like there's real leverage to use the word again and talk and like really building, building, building for that user base but in consumer SaaS., it's different.

307
00:26:05,520 --> 00:26:10,400
Like, you know, you're going to get $100 a year or whatever your number is from that user if they're way engaged.

308
00:26:10,720 --> 00:26:18,720
So unfortunately, this tragedy of the commons where you do have to make sure you build features that are going to drive mass adoption, not just deeply engaged user adoption.

309
00:26:19,280 --> 00:26:26,880
What is an example of something, one of those tactics, like you just say, like, hey, come back or do you offer anything free or like, what maybe you've tried that's worked or not?

310
00:26:27,520 --> 00:26:37,280
When I say leverage, what I really mean is about focus because I think that's just the critical driver for startup success is where we focus our limited time and resources to answer that question.

311
00:26:37,600 --> 00:26:42,880
I'll actually point to what we're focused on right now and where the leverage is, so to speak.

312
00:26:43,120 --> 00:26:45,280
So, you know, I mentioned our retention is pretty solid.

313
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:52,000
We're actually more focused on conversion and activation as a way to move short term, immediate business metrics.

314
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:59,040
And there's more leverage there in the sense that there's more users actually in the activation funnel that we could potentially convert.

315
00:26:59,360 --> 00:27:07,440
So out of like say ten thousand subscribers, there's about fifteen or twenty thousand that make it pretty far in the trial funnel and don't convert.

316
00:27:08,160 --> 00:27:12,560
And if we were able to convert that group, we could potentially like double subscribers, so to speak.

317
00:27:12,560 --> 00:27:15,760
So we're not as focused on retention right now.

318
00:27:16,320 --> 00:27:17,360
We're more focused there.

319
00:27:17,600 --> 00:27:21,920
And I can tell you a bit about how we go about trying to convert that group.

320
00:27:22,480 --> 00:27:22,560
Yeah.

321
00:27:22,880 --> 00:27:30,480
I wanted to actually throw a hypothetical your way because I think this kind of product thinking gets lost too often.

322
00:27:30,960 --> 00:27:32,640
I'm a Fitbot lifer at this point.

323
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:34,000
Like, I'm going to keep paying you.

324
00:27:34,320 --> 00:27:39,200
I mean, maybe I'd find a different workout app at some point or whatever, but you would just pay for an app because you like it so much.

325
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:39,840
I do that a lot.

326
00:27:39,920 --> 00:27:40,000
Yeah.

327
00:27:40,400 --> 00:27:44,400
If I'm not using it, I'll just be like, I want this to still be around the optionality thing.

328
00:27:44,560 --> 00:27:44,720
Right.

329
00:27:44,880 --> 00:27:44,960
So.

330
00:27:45,360 --> 00:27:46,880
But as a user, there's specific.

331
00:27:47,440 --> 00:27:48,480
features I want.

332
00:27:49,040 --> 00:27:52,400
And I think this is a trap a lot, especially consumer founders get in.

333
00:27:52,640 --> 00:27:57,280
But even, I mean, we face this at RevenueCat, you know, when a customer says, Oh, I really need this chart.

334
00:27:57,440 --> 00:28:00,320
And then it's like, Oh, well, we should drop everything and build that chart?

335
00:28:00,480 --> 00:28:01,840
Or what's the broader strategy?

336
00:28:02,240 --> 00:28:05,440
So let me throw a couple of features I personally want in FitBod.

337
00:28:05,840 --> 00:28:12,000
You tell me how you contextualize that into, you know, what's going to activate new users?

338
00:28:12,400 --> 00:28:15,040
What's going to keep existing users subscribed?

339
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:19,360
And I know you have this kind of offense versus defense framework that you think through.

340
00:28:19,920 --> 00:28:23,040
So feature pitch in my little home garage.

341
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:28,080
I have a TV that I'll like ride my airbike and watch TV while I work out.

342
00:28:28,720 --> 00:28:30,400
I would love an Apple TV app.

343
00:28:30,720 --> 00:28:31,920
Jesse, build that for me.

344
00:28:32,080 --> 00:28:33,680
It's the next great FitBod feature.

345
00:28:33,840 --> 00:28:34,400
It's amazing.

346
00:28:34,960 --> 00:28:43,840
Now, where do you go from there as a product leader to contextualize that feature request and decide whether or not it's something worth focusing on?

347
00:28:44,240 --> 00:28:45,760
There's a few different levels of context.

348
00:28:46,080 --> 00:28:47,040
I'll try to be concise.

349
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:52,240
I mean, at the highest level, we're going to prioritize based on our vision and the steps we need to get there.

350
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:58,960
So we're aiming to enable 90 percent of adults to like realize the value of physical fitness and in particular strength training.

351
00:28:59,200 --> 00:29:07,280
And so like step one there is to establish product market fit in this core segment and I'm going to call it the ideal customer profile or ICP.

352
00:29:07,920 --> 00:29:10,960
These are people who exhibit strong conversion and retention today.

353
00:29:11,760 --> 00:29:16,560
Now step two, which I think we're in now, is to try to scale and own the market for the ICP.

354
00:29:17,040 --> 00:29:20,240
And step three is to, you know, basically build for the non ICP.

355
00:29:20,480 --> 00:29:26,560
So at this point we're taking the lens of we want to increase ICP acquisition, activation and retention.

356
00:29:26,960 --> 00:29:28,720
And so moving into offense versus.

357
00:29:29,200 --> 00:29:30,880
defense is like the next piece of context.

358
00:29:31,600 --> 00:29:35,600
This is a great framework developed by Eli Lerner at Reforge.

359
00:29:36,080 --> 00:29:38,880
You can google offense versus defense and find a great blog post.

360
00:29:39,120 --> 00:29:40,400
I won't describe as much today.

361
00:29:41,200 --> 00:29:49,760
So defense is about maintaining product market fit and preventing downside risk, competition, I don't know, tech debt and kind of the steps to achieve the vision.

362
00:29:50,240 --> 00:29:53,360
And offense is about the immediate short term business metrics.

363
00:29:53,760 --> 00:30:00,240
In our offense right now, our focus is not preventing churn, it's not filling the top of the funnel, it's about driving conversion.

364
00:30:00,560 --> 00:30:10,480
And so what we're researching, or we have been last year is to understand why the ICP is not converting via user research, deploying some tests, right?

365
00:30:10,720 --> 00:30:13,760
What is the most constraining problem for why the ICP is not converting?

366
00:30:14,240 --> 00:30:19,120
Is that because of the lack of an Apple TV app or whatever the problem that solution solves?

367
00:30:19,360 --> 00:30:23,760
Well, when you say it that way, or even on deep.

368
00:30:24,240 --> 00:30:24,640
So key.

369
00:30:24,800 --> 00:30:24,880
Yeah.

370
00:30:25,520 --> 00:30:25,920
Key, right?

371
00:30:26,480 --> 00:30:36,000
I think there's a trap of thinking that let's listen to like our super successful engaged customers as kind of a clue for what the unsuccessful customers are, are missing.

372
00:30:36,560 --> 00:30:40,160
That that's not it's the planes with the bullet holes picture that you see all the time, right?

373
00:30:40,320 --> 00:30:45,440
It's like if you talk to all the ones where they examine the planes coming back from France and they're like, oh, well, look at all where they get shot.

374
00:30:45,680 --> 00:30:46,720
But actually it's the opposite.

375
00:30:46,880 --> 00:30:48,000
You're talking to people who made it.

376
00:30:48,160 --> 00:30:48,800
Don't talk to them.

377
00:30:49,200 --> 00:30:49,600
They made it.

378
00:30:50,320 --> 00:30:51,360
David's already engaged.

379
00:30:51,680 --> 00:30:52,800
You're going to be happy with your app.

380
00:30:53,120 --> 00:30:54,480
David, I don't care anymore.

381
00:30:54,640 --> 00:30:55,840
That's not true, obviously.

382
00:30:56,000 --> 00:31:00,800
But yeah, I think the tragedy is those are so much harder conversations to have because that user is inherently gone.

383
00:31:01,120 --> 00:31:04,000
So like capturing them on the way out, I think is a real challenge.

384
00:31:04,560 --> 00:31:05,920
So how do you have those conversations?

385
00:31:06,320 --> 00:31:14,560
So you take the Apple TV and that one maybe is too easy to just say, well, that's obviousiously a pretty niche thing for someone to be in a home gym.

386
00:31:14,880 --> 00:31:18,640
And you probably have some data to just write that off off off of your head.

387
00:31:19,200 --> 00:31:31,680
But what does it look like to actually dig into that focus area of activation and not let your super users like me distract you from the things that are going to matter for activation?

388
00:31:32,240 --> 00:31:33,440
User research is critical.

389
00:31:33,600 --> 00:31:44,960
So there's a quantitative approach where we'll try to compare this nonconvert cohort against our successful subscribers and note any differences in their profile characteristics or like the actions in the trial experience.

390
00:31:45,280 --> 00:31:52,640
